WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:03.840
Every term, students ask Professor Lee the same question.

00:00:03.890 --> 00:00:06.140
Can I use AI for this assignment?

00:00:06.190 --> 00:00:09.100
A single rule for the whole course leaves them guessing.

00:00:10.150 --> 00:00:13.780
The answer depends on what each assignment is meant to teach.

00:00:13.830 --> 00:00:15.660
So start with the learning goal.

00:00:16.706 --> 00:00:18.596
Then choose one of four levels.

00:00:18.646 --> 00:00:22.316
Level zero: no AI, when the skill itself is the goal.

00:00:22.366 --> 00:00:24.746
Level one: AI for ideas.

00:00:24.796 --> 00:00:28.426
Level two: AI for feedback on the student's own draft.

00:00:28.476 --> 00:00:33.886
Level three: AI as a collaborator, with notes on what the student asked and changed.

00:00:34.935 --> 00:00:38.595
Put each level in your syllabus and in every Canvas assignment.

00:00:38.645 --> 00:00:42.295
Then ask students to add a short note whenever they use AI.

00:00:43.347 --> 00:00:47.067
If a detector flags an essay, treat the score as one signal.

00:00:47.117 --> 00:00:51.157
Look at drafts and version history, and talk with the student about the work.

00:00:52.207 --> 00:00:55.697
Clear rules help students learn, and use AI honestly.

00:00:55.747 --> 00:00:58.357
Start your plan with the AI in Your Course kit.
